## Context

The brain vault is the pipeline's durable memory — research agents write
domain findings into it, QA agents promote retros, and the coding playbook
captures engineering conventions that survive across projects. Without it,
agents that declare Obsidian MCP access silently lose the ability to persist
or retrieve knowledge, and the pipeline cannot learn across runs.

The original provisionBrain created directories and wired the MCP server,
but left the vault content-empty. This meant a new user's first
study-playbook invocation found an empty coding-playbook folder with no hub
note, returning nothing. Their agents had no vault CLAUDE.md to teach them
how to navigate the brain. The user had to manually create these files —
an invisible prerequisite that contradicted init's promise of "one command
to a working AIDE project."

The fix is to scaffold content alongside structure. Two templates make the
vault immediately agent-navigable: a coding-playbook hub with the section
structure the study-playbook skill expects, and a vault-root CLAUDE.md with
the crawling and decision protocols agents need to interact with the brain.
Both are structurally complete but content-empty — they establish the
pattern without fabricating domain-specific knowledge the user hasn't
created yet.

The planner-only constraint is load-bearing. provisionBrain returns
InitStep items describing what to create; the init orchestrator's apply
layer handles actual writes. This separation means provisionBrain never
touches the filesystem, the agent has full visibility into what will be
created, and the user confirms before anything is written. Content
templates are just InitStep items with a content field — they flow through
the same planning and apply pipeline as directory scaffolding.

## Strategy

**Four planning steps, not two.** The current implementation returns two
steps (vault directories, MCP prescription). The expanded version returns
up to four: vault directories, playbook hub template, vault CLAUDE.md,
and MCP prescription. Each is its own InitStep with independent
idempotency — if the vault exists but the playbook hub is missing, only
the hub step reports would-create.

**Content steps use the same InitStep shape as directory steps.** The
playbook hub and vault CLAUDE.md are InitStep items with category "brain",
a filePath pointing to the target location inside the vault, and a content
field containing the template text. The apply layer already knows how to
write content from InitStep — no new machinery needed.

**Playbook hub structure is enforced, content is empty.** The template
contains five sections in a fixed order: Task Routing (a Markdown table
with header row and no data rows), How to Use This Index (navigation
instructions), Always Read First (placeholder wikilinks for conventions
and folder structure), Sections (empty numbered list), and Contents (empty
flat list). This structure is what the study-playbook skill's crawling
protocol expects to find. The empty-but-structured pattern means the skill
works on day one — it finds the hub, reads the sections, and correctly
reports that no playbook notes exist yet.

**Vault CLAUDE.md is scoped to navigation, not project instructions.** The
template covers four concerns: wikilink crawling protocol (hub vs content
notes, depth counting, never re-read), decision protocol (coding
conventions route through study-playbook, everything else through
search_notes), a "where to find things" table mapping information types
to vault paths, and a brief statement of the brain's role in AIDE. It
does not include project-specific instructions, user preferences, or
anything that belongs in the user's global or project CLAUDE.md.

**Idempotency checks are per-file, not per-vault.** The existing vault
check (non-empty directory or .obsidian present) gates the directory
scaffolding step. The playbook hub, vault CLAUDE.md, and MCP prescription
each have their own existence check — if the target is present, that step
reports "exists" regardless of the vault-level status. This means running
init on a vault that has directories but lost its playbook hub will
correctly re-plan only the missing file.

**Two different idempotency semantics coexist in this module — know which
file uses which, and why.** The coding-playbook hub and vault-root
CLAUDE.md use **seed-semantic idempotency**: a simple presence check at
the expected path. If the file exists, the step is "exists" — full stop.
Never "would-overwrite," never a byte comparison against the bundled
template. These files are scaffolds the user evolves the moment the vault
comes alive; the playbook hub gains routing entries and wikilinks, the
vault CLAUDE.md may pick up project-specific navigation notes. Once the
user has the file, the bytes belong to the user and are out of scope for
comparison. The Obsidian MCP prescription, by contrast, uses
**canonical-template idempotency**: it is a config value the tool owns,
so byte-identity is the right signal — if the on-disk command string
drifts from the platform-aware prescription, the step reports
"would-overwrite" because the tool is the source of truth for that
entry. The distinguishing question is *who owns the bytes after first
write*: for user-curated content, presence is enough; for
tool-prescribed config, identity matters. Getting this wrong is not a
cosmetic bug — a would-overwrite status on the playbook hub is one
Enter-press away from wiping the user's curated conventions and
replacing them with an empty scaffold.

**Template text lives in the helper, not in external files.** The playbook
hub and CLAUDE.md templates are string literals inside provisionBrain.
They are small (under 2KB each), change only when the AIDE methodology
changes, and are consumed by exactly one caller. Externalizing them to
separate template files would add indirection without reducing coupling.

## Good examples

A new vault is being scaffolded. provisionBrain returns four InitStep
items:

Step 1 — Vault directories. Status: would-create. Category: brain.
Content: JSON array of directory paths to create.

Step 2 — Playbook hub. Status: would-create. Category: brain. FilePath
points to coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md inside the vault. Content
is a Markdown file with five headings: Task Routing (table with headers,
no rows), How to Use This Index, Always Read First, Sections (empty),
Contents (empty). Every heading is present; no domain content is filled.

Step 3 — Vault CLAUDE.md. Status: would-create. Category: brain. FilePath
points to CLAUDE.md at the vault root. Content covers crawling protocol,
decision protocol, where-to-find-things table, and AIDE brain role. No
project-specific instructions appear.

Step 4 — Obsidian MCP. Status: would-create. Category: mcp. Prescription
contains the server key and platform-aware command entry.

Re-running on the same vault returns all four steps as "exists." The
directory step sees the expected folders. The playbook hub and vault
CLAUDE.md are detected by a presence check at their expected paths —
their on-disk bytes are never compared to the bundled templates. The MCP
prescription sees the obsidian key already present in the MCP config
with a matching command entry. No content is regenerated, no files are
touched.

Running on a vault where the user has evolved the playbook hub — added
routing entries, wikilinked several real playbook notes, customized the
"Always Read First" section — still returns the playbook hub step as
"exists." The user's curated bytes are preserved; provisionBrain has no
opinion on what the file contains beyond the fact that it is there.

Running on a vault that exists but is missing the playbook hub (perhaps
the user deleted it) returns: directories as "exists," playbook hub as
"would-create," CLAUDE.md as "exists," MCP as "exists." Only the missing
piece is re-planned.

## Bad examples

A playbook hub template that contains fabricated section entries like
"## Section 1: TypeScript Conventions" with wikilinks to notes that do
not exist. The user has not created any playbook content yet — invented
sections mislead agents into thinking conventions exist when they do not.

A vault CLAUDE.md that includes the user's personal configuration, like
"You are helping a data scientist" or "This project uses Prisma." The
vault CLAUDE.md is a generic navigation guide, not a project or user
profile. Mixing concerns means every project sharing the vault inherits
instructions meant for one project.

A provisionBrain that checks only "does the vault directory exist" and
returns all content steps as "exists" even when the playbook hub file is
missing. Per-vault idempotency is too coarse — each content file needs
its own existence check, or the user cannot recover from a partially
scaffolded vault without deleting everything and starting over.

A provisionBrain that writes the playbook hub to disk directly instead of
returning it as an InitStep. This bypasses the agent's confirmation flow,
meaning the user never sees or approves the content before it lands in
their vault. The planner-only contract exists precisely to prevent this.

A provisionBrain that reads the existing playbook hub off disk,
byte-compares it to the bundled template, and — when the user has added
routing rows and wikilinks — reports the step as "would-overwrite." The
init wizard then prompts "overwrite existing playbook hub? [y/N]," and a
reflexive Enter replaces months of curated conventions with a bare
scaffold. This is the exact failure the seed-semantic rule exists to
prevent: byte identity is the wrong signal for files the user owns
post-scaffold. The same failure shape applies to the vault-root
CLAUDE.md — any byte-comparison branch on these two files is a bug,
regardless of how the drift is detected.

